• @predictor.What-If Thought: ## Summary of Research

  I confirmed via `get_transactions` (team=PHI) that on 2026-06-02 (week 23) the Eagles "Traded WR A.J. Brown to New England for a 2028 first-round pick and a conditional 2027 fifth-round pick." This is the real, current-season baseline state. I also confirmed via the same tool that Philadelphia released OC Kevin Patullo (2026-01-13), and WebSearch (corroborated across CBS Philly, Yahoo, NBC Sports Philadelphia, and Bleeding Green Nation) confirms his replacement is Sean Mannion, a first-time NFL coordinator — the Eagles' fifth OC in five seasons. A single-source WebSearch result (ClutchPoints) references rumored "Hurts-Brown" friction; I treat that as unconfirmed and did not weight it heavily.

  For contender identification, I pulled 2025 `get_team_seasonal_stats` for all 16 NFC teams and ranked by passing EPA (offense proxy) and sacks+interceptions (defense proxy). That surfaced LA, GB, DAL, DET, SF, CHI, SEA, PHI on offense and ATL, CHI, SEA, MIN, LA, DET, PHI, NO on defense — the composite group I then deep-dived with `get_transactions` (all confirmed structured data): LA traded LB Jared Verse + three future picks to Cleveland for Myles Garrett and extended McVay/Snead; SF lost WR Ricky Pearsall to season-ending IR but signed Deebo Samuel and Mike Evans; DET cut starting CB Terrion Arnold and has Brian Branch/Kerby Joseph/Tyler Conklin on the PUP list; GB has Micah Parsons (currently PUP) and hired Jonathan Gannon as DC, but traded away Rashan Gary to Dallas; CHI promoted Press Taylor to OC and released LB Tremaine Edmunds. `get_injury_report` and `get_coaching_changes`/`get_management_changes` returned "no data source available" for every team I checked, so all injury/coaching/management claims above are WebSearch-sourced, not tool-confirmed structured data (I've flagged that difference in the explanation).

  WebSearch consensus (sportsbook aggregators, cross-checked across two sources) has the Rams as clear NFC favorites (+300, ~25% implied) after the Garrett trade, Seahawks next (+525, ~16%, reported as reigning NFC champs), then a tight middle tier of PHI/DET/SF/DAL/GB (~8-10% implied each) and CHI (~7%), with the rest of the conference sharing the remaining single digits — this lines up closely with my own offense/defense proxy rankings, which increases my confidence in the shape of the distribution.

